P18-V01 strict batch decoder public-surface correction —
2026-10-22 21:34 UTC

Environment: macOS AArch64, Apple M5 Max (18 logical CPUs), Rust 1.85.0.
Branch: `phase/18-phase-18-compatibility-closure`. Pinned RocketMQ Java
client/source: 5.5.1, commit `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`.

Implementation commit: `95525c06 fix(common): hide non-Java strict batch APIs`.
Reverse API review found that Common exported `decode_many` and
`decode_many_as_client`, which returned strict errors for malformed batch
records although pinned Java only exposes prefix-preserving batch methods.
No production owner used the strict exports. The exports and wrappers are
removed; production/test/fuzz callers now use the Java-compatible batch APIs.
Strict decode failure details remain private inside the Common decoder. The
Common aggregate-limit unit test and malformed-tail internal test retain
coverage of safety accounting and private failure detection.

Validation on the implementation tree:
- `cargo test --offline --workspace --locked --quiet` and
  `cargo test --offline --workspace --all-features --locked --quiet` — PASS.
- Strict all-target/all-feature workspace Clippy, formatting, and diff check —
  PASS.
- `cargo doc --offline -p rocketmq-classic-common --all-features --no-deps
  --locked` — PASS. Generated public Common decoder docs include the Java-mapped
  compatible methods and no longer list `decode_many` or
  `decode_many_as_client`.
- Differential self-test and manifest validation — PASS (37 scenarios).
- Bounded fuzz smoke — PASS (163 frame seeds, 11 message seeds, 2,784
  mutations, 245 frame decodes, 192 message decodes).
- `mvn -o -q -f compat/java-reference/pom.xml verify` — PASS; 37 scenarios,
  11 message decoder fixtures, 36 route/VIP cases, 106 typed headers, and 19
  typed bodies.

This closes this Rust-only public decoder surface mismatch only. Broad P18-V01
API/settings/source/input/error/control-flow and reverse-export reconciliation,
